{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 3,
  "simulation_id": "sim-02",
  "member_id": "pearce-mark",
  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B041",
        "laureates": [
          "Francis Halzen"
        ],
        "citation": "For establishing high-energy neutrino astronomy with IceCube"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B003",
    "B011"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "IceCube established that high-energy neutrinos arrive from beyond the Solar System, opening an observational window that complements light and charged cosmic rays. This is a major experimental achievement: a detector built to work in the Antarctic ice made it possible to identify a diffuse astrophysical neutrino flux and begin connecting neutrinos to cosmic accelerators. Halzen's central role in conceiving and realizing IceCube supports a focused individual attribution, while the collaboration's contribution must be acknowledged in assessing that credit.",
    "case_against": "The discovery rests on a large collaboration and a massive detector, so attributing it to one person risks underrepresenting the instrument builders and analysts. The dominant sources and acceleration mechanisms behind the observed flux also remain unsettled. Those are serious reservations about attribution and interpretation, even though the existence of the astrophysical neutrino signal is established.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "You emphasized that the astrophysical flux is supported by years of data, while the dominant source populations remain unidentified.",
        "response": "I agree that the source question is unresolved and should temper the citation. I would distinguish it from the discovery at issue: establishing a high-energy astrophysical neutrino flux is itself a durable result, even before the source population is fully mapped."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "You argued that WMAP sharply constrained cosmological parameters and that its results remain foundational, while stressing the need to separate mission leaders' credit from the wider collaboration.",
        "response": "That is a strong case, and the collaboration-credit concern applies to IceCube as well. I favor IceCube here because it opened a distinct observational channel in astroparticle physics; WMAP's impact is formidable, but its place in the longer sequence of CMB measurements makes the prize boundary harder to define."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "How decisively Halzen's individual leadership can be distinguished from the contributions of IceCube's international collaboration.",
      "How much the unresolved identification of the dominant neutrino sources should affect the assessment of the discovery's lasting significance."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
